Let $\Delta_{\rm d}$ be the nonnegative discrete Laplacian on $\mathbb Z^d$. We prove that, for $0<p\le1$, \[
  \|\Delta_{\rm d}^{i\gamma}\|_{H^p_{\Delta_{\rm d}}\to H^p_{\Delta_{\rm d}}}
  \simeq (1+|\gamma|)^{d(1/p-1/2)}, \] and, for $1<p<\infty$, \[
  \|\Delta_{\rm d}^{i\gamma}\|_{\ell^p\to\ell^p}
  \simeq (1+|\gamma|)^{d|1/p-1/2|}. \] The estimates are sharp with respect to the exponent of $1+|\gamma|$.
  We also explain why the general spectral multiplier theorem and the usual Calder\'on--Zygmund approach do not give the sharp power.

Let $X$ be a complex klt Fano variety. We study the rational total index $\tau_X$ and the nef complexity $c_X=\dim X+\rho(X)-\tau_X$. Previous joint work of the author with Enwright, Filipazzi, Gongyo, Moraga, Svaldi, and Wang established the corresponding nonnegativity and zero-case characterization in the broader framework of a special class of generalized log Calabi--Yau pairs. Here we give a direct proof in the Fano setting, prove that there is no value strictly between zero and one, and classify all klt Fano varieties with $c_X<2$, without assuming $\mathbb Q$-factoriality. At complexity one, after splitting off projective-space factors, the remaining factor is a normal quadric of Picard number one, a linear blow-up of projective space, or an integral $(1,1)$ hypersurface in a product of projective spaces. If $1<c_X<2$, only two weighted-projective families occur; in particular, no smooth Fano variety has nef complexity strictly between one and two. We also determine the spectrum when the dimension is allowed to vary: \[
  \{0\}\cup\left\{2-\frac2q \ \middle| \ q\in\mathbb Z_{\geq2}\right\}
  \cup(\mathbb Q\cap[2,\infty)). \] Most proofs in this paper were developed from arguments generated by OpenAI's ChatGPT through discussions initiated and guided by the author; see the statement on the use of generative AI.

Let $p_d=(d^2+1)/(d(d-1))$ and $q_d=(d^2+1)/(d-1)$. We prove that the local spherical maximal operator $\sup_{1\leq t\leq 2}|f*d\sigma_t|$ is bounded from $L^{p_d}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ to $L^{q_d}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ for every $d\geq 2$. Previously only the restricted weak type estimate was known at this endpoint. Together with known results, this completes the characterization of the strong type $L^p\to L^q$ bounds for the local spherical maximal operator. We also prove the maximal estimate \[
  \Big\|\sup_{t\in\mathbb{R}}|e^{it\sqrt{-\Delta}}f|\Big\|_{L^{2(d+1)/(d-1)}(\mathbb{R}^d)}
  \lesssim\|f\|_{\dot H^{d/(d+1)}(\mathbb{R}^d)} \] for the wave equation at the critical exponent. Both results are obtained by exploiting the gain in bilinear estimates that comes from the interaction between high and low frequencies.

We characterize the following three classes of ordered trees:
  $\qquad$ (1) trees $T$ satisfying $R_<(T,K_n)=O(n)$;
  $\qquad$ (2) $\chi$-bounding trees;
  $\qquad$ (3) Ramsey-good trees.
  In particular, we show that the first two classes coincide. The second characterization answers a question of Axenovich, Rollin and Ueckerdt, and the third resolves a problem of Balko and Poljak.
  In our proof, we also introduce several novel constructions of ordered graphs with sublinear independence number, giving lower bounds on $R_<(F,K_n)$ for various ordered forests $F$.

We investigate the qualitative properties of positive classical solutions $u$ to critical Hartree equations on punctured domains, assuming that $u(x)\to+\infty$ as $x\to0$. For the global problem set in $\mathbb R^n\setminus\{0\}$, we prove that such solutions are radial and nonincreasing in $|x|$. For the local problem in $B_R\setminus\{0\}$, we establish the upper bound $u(x)\leqslant C|x|^{-(n-2)/2}$ near the origin and the asymptotic radial symmetry estimate
  \begin{equation*}
  u(x)=\bar u(|x|)\bigl(1+\mathcal O(|x|)\bigr)\qquad\text{as }x\to0,
  \end{equation*}
  where $\bar u$ is the spherical average of $u$. Our approach combines integral representations, blow-up analysis, and the method of moving spheres. A main difficulty is controlling the nonlocal Hartree potential under Kelvin inversion and in the blow-up limit. We also give an alternative proof of the classification of positive entire classical solutions in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$, which is used to identify the blow-up profile.

